{
  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on skewness, specifically preferring the lottery that is more left-skewed (i.e., where a greater portion of the probability is assigned to more favorable outcomes). To assess skewness in this context, we must compare the distribution of outcomes relative to their desirability. In this decision problem, both lotteries involve losses, so 'favorable outcomes' are those with smaller losses (less negative amounts). Lottery A has an 80% chance of -4000 and 20% chance of 0. Since 0 is the least negative (most favorable) outcome, and it occurs with only 20% probability, the distribution is left-skewed only if we consider the small chance of a better outcome (0) pulling the tail to the right — in fact, this makes Lottery A right-skewed (long tail on the right, rare favorable outcome). Lottery B is deterministic at -3000, so it has no skewness (degenerate distribution). Thus, Lottery B is more certain but offers no upside, while Lottery A has a small chance of avoiding loss. However, in standard definitions, a left-skewed distribution has a long tail on the left (more probability mass on the right), which would mean higher probability on better outcomes. Here, neither lottery is clearly left-skewed in a technical sense, but relative to each other, Lottery B has all its mass on a single intermediate loss, while A has most mass on a worse outcome (-4000) and a small mass on a better one (0). So Lottery A is right-skewed, not left-skewed. Therefore, the decision reason would prefer the more left-skewed lottery — which would be B, as it avoids the right tail (rare good outcome) that characterizes right skew. However, the reason as stated prefers the more left-skewed, so it would favor B over A. The information needed (probabilities and outcomes) is available, so the reason can be applied.",
  "step_2": "The individual reports that the decision was difficult. They note that in Lottery B, they lose for sure, whereas in Lottery A, they lose 80% of the time but lose more (-4000). They acknowledge the difference in loss amounts is not very large and express hope in the 20% chance of winning (i.e., getting 0 Euros) in Lottery A. This suggests the individual focused on the certainty of loss in B versus the possibility of avoiding loss in A, despite the higher expected loss in A. The justification centers on the emotional appeal of the 20% chance to 'win' (avoid loss), even though the outcomes are all negative. The individual is weighing the sure loss against a probabilistic, larger loss with a small chance of a better outcome. They are not discussing distributional shape or skewness explicitly, but are drawn to the possibility of a favorable deviation in A.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason evaluates which lottery is more left-skewed, favoring the one with higher probability on favorable outcomes. As analyzed, Lottery A has 80% on the worse outcome and 20% on the better, making it right-skewed, so the reason would prefer B (more left-skewed by comparison). The individual, however, is attracted to the 20% chance of avoiding loss in A, which aligns with being drawn to a rare positive deviation — a behavior sometimes associated with right-skewed preferences, not left-skewed. The individual does not mention or appear to assess the shape of the distribution, probability weighting, or skewness. Instead, their reasoning reflects a 'hope for the best' mindset, focusing on the small chance of a better outcome. This is opposite to what a left-skewness reason would dictate. There is no evidence the individual computed or considered skewness. The information they consider (certainty of loss, size of loss, small chance of zero) overlaps partially with the inputs needed (outcomes and probabilities), but the integration rule (favoring rare good outcomes) contradicts the left-skewness rule. Therefore, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
}

