  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on skewness, defined as the relative probability mass on favorable versus unfavorable outcomes, and prefers the more left-skewed lottery. In this case, Lottery B has a higher probability (55%) of the best possible outcome (0) compared to Lottery A (10%), making B more left-skewed. The individual considers exactly this: the higher probability of avoiding a loss (i.e., achieving the favorable outcome of 0) in B (55%) versus A (10%), and explicitly uses this as the justification for their choice, even acknowledging the larger loss magnitude in B. Although the term 'skewness' is not used, the individual's reasoning aligns with the core logic of the decision reason—favoring the lottery with higher probability on the better outcome. The focus on the likelihood of 'winning' (i.e., not losing) and downplaying the size of the loss matches the skewness-based rule. Therefore, the individual's deliberation reflects the use of the skewness-based decision reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 100
}

{
  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on skewness, which depends on the distribution of probabilities across favorable and unfavorable outcomes. In this decision problem, both lotteries involve only zero or negative outcomes (losses or no loss), so there are no positive (favorable) outcomes. Lottery A has a 0.2% chance of a -3000 loss and 99.8% chance of 0; Lottery B has a 0.1% chance of a -6000 loss and 99.9% chance of 0. Since all non-zero outcomes are losses (unfavorable), a 'favorable outcome' in this context would be 0 (no loss). Thus, the probability of a favorable outcome is 99.8% for A and 99.9% for B. Therefore, B assigns a greater probability to the favorable outcome (0) than A, making B more left-skewed. The decision reason would prefer Lottery B as it is more left-skewed. The reason can be applied to this problem.",
  "step_2": "The individual states: 'Ich möchte das Risiko zu verlieren so klein wie möglich halten, bei 0.01 ist es sehr unwahrscheinlich dass ich verliere' (I want to keep the risk of losing as small as possible; at 0.01, it's very unlikely that I lose). This indicates that the individual focuses on minimizing the probability of any loss. The mention of '0.01' likely refers to 0.01 (i.e., 1%), but given the context, it seems to approximate the small probabilities involved (0.1% or 0.2%). The individual emphasizes wanting the chance of losing to be as low as possible. The justification centers on minimizing the likelihood of a loss, not on the magnitude of the loss or the skewness per se, but on the probability of avoiding a loss altogether. The individual values a lower chance of loss, aligning with preferring the lottery with the smallest probability of a non-zero outcome.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason evaluates which lottery is more left-skewed, i.e., assigns higher probability to favorable (non-loss) outcomes. In this case, Lottery B has a 99.9% chance of no loss, compared to 99.8% for A, so B is more left-skewed and would be preferred by the reason. The individual also focuses on minimizing the probability of loss, expressing a desire to keep it 'as small as possible'. This aligns with focusing on the probability of the favorable outcome (no loss), which is the basis of skewness in this context. Although the individual does not explicitly mention skewness or use statistical terminology, the core of their reasoning — maximizing the chance of no loss — matches the informational basis of the decision reason. The justification, while phrased in terms of risk avoidance, effectively corresponds to preferring the more left-skewed lottery. Therefore, the individual's reasoning is consistent with the decision reason's rule.",
  "final_assessment_score": 90
